 Ohle Claussen <claussen@dpi.physik.uni-goettingen.de> wrote:
> A magic lamp is _not_ a good source of an emergency wish, as it takes
> several turns to get one. I would not be surprised if telling an
> unexperienced player who finds a magic lamp in, say, minetown (which isn't
> too uncommon) to convert it to appropriate DSM (taking into account the
> usual arguments about reflection from Soko etc.) immediately upped their
> statistics significantly more than keeping it as a light source.

How often are emergency wishes useful anyway?  When I die (even back 
when I was a newbie) it's usually either an instadeath or a situation 
where I don't have time to realize the benefits of a wished-for item.

I suppose a wand of death or rubber chicken might be a useful emergency 
wish in some situations.

I think that if you're going to use an early magic lamp, *DSM is the 
logical choice.  Low-weight AC ups the survivability of a young 
character dramatically--especially one played by a newbie who isn't 
adept at avoiding combat.

I'm not a meticulous enough player to ascend most of my games.  The 
tipping point for the ones which I do ascend seems to be acquiring 
enough AC; once I have enough that I'm not in danger of death-by-ant, 
making it to the castle is mainly a matter of not making any mistakes.  
And to my recollection, I've never lost a game after getting the castle 
wand; YASD is really the only danger from that point onwards.

